<node id="art:b230bbff6a09">
  <data key="d0">ArtForm</data>
  <data key="d14">Dhak, Dhol and Kansar</data>
  <data key="d15">ঢাকের বাদ্যি</data>
  <data key="d51">1606</data>
  <data key="d52">Thriving</data>
  <data key="d9">Indian Institute of Science Bengaluru</data>
</node>
<node id="art:57d4fe1363b9">
  <data key="d0">ArtForm</data>
  <data key="d14">Pratima Shilpa (Idol Making)</data>
  <data key="d15">প্রতিমা শিল্প</data>
  <data key="d51">Sculpture</data>
  <data key="d52">Thriving</data>
  <data key="d9">The idol-maker's year begins on Rath Yatra, when the previous year's bamboo frame is worshipped and reused. At many old family pujas the same kathamo has carried the goddess for over a century, the skeleton persists, only the skin is remade.</data>
</node>
<node id="art:449b21a20c14">
  <data key="d0">ArtForm</data>
  <data key="d14">Krishnanagar Clay Dolls</data>
  <data key="d15">কৃষ্ণনগরের মাটির পুতুল</data>
  <data key="d51">Sculpture</data>
  <data key="d52">Evolving</data>
  <data key="d9">The Ghurni cooperative applied for a Geographical Indication in 2021 and the Registry approved it as the Krishnagar Clay Doll in July 2026, after decades in which the name was borrowed by mass-produced imitations. Over 300 people in Ghurni work in clay.</data>
</node>
<node id="art:e399ebd9456f">
  <data key="d0">ArtForm</data>
  <data key="d14">Daker Saj</data>
  <data key="d15">ডাকের সাজ</data>
  <data key="d51">Craft</data>
  <data key="d52"></data>
  <data key="d9">Daker saj is one of the few Indian craft names that is an accidental fossil of global trade, it records a supply chain running from a German foil mill to a Calcutta post office to a potter's lane.</data>
</node>
<node id="art:8576a321caf4">
  <data key="d0">ArtForm</data>
  <data key="d14">Sholar Saj (Sholapith)</data>
  <data key="d15">শোলার সাজ</data>
  <data key="d51">Craft</data>
  <data key="d52"></data>
  <data key="d9">The same craft dresses both ends of a Bengali life, the shola topor and mukut worn by a bride and groom at a wedding, and the white ornament that goes into the river with the goddess.</data>
</node>
<node id="art:101081b2bd48">
  <data key="d0">ArtForm</data>
  <data key="d14">Chalchitra (Painted Backdrop)</data>
  <data key="d15">চালচিত্র</data>
  <data key="d51">Painting</data>
  <data key="d52"></data>
  <data key="d9">The chalchitra has escaped the idol. Theme pujas now hire patuas to paint chalchitra logic across an entire pandal, walls, ceiling and corridor, so visitors walk inside the backdrop instead of looking at it.</data>
</node>
<node id="art:39521821a49f">
  <data key="d0">ArtForm</data>
  <data key="d14">Patachitra and Pater Gaan</data>
  <data key="d15">পটচিত্র ও পটের গান</data>
  <data key="d51">Painting</data>
  <data key="d52">Thriving</data>
  <data key="d9">The patuas of Naya are Muslim by faith and bear the title Chitrakar, and they paint and sing Hindu mythology for a living. Bengal Patachitra received its Geographical Indication on 28 March 2018.</data>
</node>
<node id="art:c294cbc14f51">
  <data key="d0">ArtForm</data>
  <data key="d14">Alpona (Ritual Floor Art)</data>
  <data key="d15">আলপনা</data>
  <data key="d51">Painting</data>
  <data key="d52">Thriving</data>
  <data key="d9">Alpona is not rangoli. Rangoli is dry, coloured powder on a grid of dots; alpona is wet, white and freehand. In 2019 a 1.4 km alpona along Lake Road in south Kolkata was billed as the world's longest street-art installation.</data>
</node>
<node id="merch:5f20be7bf44e">
  <data key="d0">Merchandise</data>
  <data key="d1">Pujo Hits IISc T-shirt</data>
  <data key="d53">পুজোর অফিসিয়াল টি-শার্ট</data>
  <data key="d42">₹449</data>
  <data key="d54">Petrol Blue</data>
  <data key="d9">Durga in white alpona with floral border and committee crest on the chest, দেবী in gold on the back, and title sponsor marks on the sleeves.</data>
  <data key="d55">Meme Theory</data>
  <data key="d56">https://www.memetheory.in/product/6aca697a15387416873f0e0d</data>
  <data key="d57">Free delivery to IISc hostel blocks, or collect at Aryabhatta or NGH. Campus orders only.</data>
  <data key="d58">Meme Theory does not take cancellations or returns once an order is placed.</data>
  <data key="d59">['S', 'M', 'L', 'XL', '2XL', '3XL']</data>
  <data key="d60">['Regular', 'Oversized']</data>
</node>
<node id="mag:6d627002f6b6">
  <data key="d0">Publication</data>
  <data key="d61">Probash</data>
  <data key="d47">প্রবাস</data>
  <data key="d62">The Annual Sharadiya Magazine of IISc Sharodiya Durgotsab</data>
  <data key="d9">Bengal invented the Sharadiya number, the annual literary issue published for the Puja. Probash is IISc's own annual literary magazine.</data>
  <data key="d63">Annual cover design competition open to all mediums (painting, digital, graphic). Chosen cover is printed on the physical magazine.</data>
  <data key="d64">Outlives the five days of puja; physical print that stays on shelves for years; allows quiet creative contributions.</data>
  <data key="d65">['Prose', 'Poetry', 'Memoir', 'Photo essays', 'Illustration', 'Translation']</data>
</node>
<node id="sound:00a34c01beca">
  <data key="d0">SoundscapeLayer</data>
  <data key="d61">The barrel drum itself, slung from the shoulder and struck with two canes. Uploaded to Wikimedia by a Bengali contributor who recorded it for exactly this purpose: to show what a dhak sounds like at a Durga Puja. Two minutes of it, which is long enough that the loop stops announcing itself.</data>
  <data key="d66">CC BY-SA 4.0 The barrel drum itself, slung from the shoulder and struck with two canes. Uploaded to Wikimedia by a Bengali contributor who recorded it for exactly this purpose: to show what a dhak sounds like at a Durga Puja. Two minutes of it, which is long enough that the loop stops announcing itself. “Rhythm of Dhak…” by Sumita Roy Dutta, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0</data>
  <data key="d9">ঢাক CC BY-SA 4.0 The barrel drum itself, slung from the shoulder and struck with two canes. Uploaded to Wikimedia by a Bengali contributor who recorded it for exactly this purpose: to show what a dhak sounds like at a Durga Puja. Two minutes of it, which is long enough that the loop stops announcing itself. “Rhythm of Dhak…” by Sumita Roy Dutta, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0</data>
</node>
<node id="sound:f35ad8957e8b">
  <data key="d0">SoundscapeLayer</data>
  <data key="d61">A struck bell-metal gong, standing in for the kansar, the flat disc beaten during arati. It is not a kansar and we are not going to pretend it is. No recording of one exists under any licence we can use.</data>
  <data key="d66"></data>
  <data key="d9">কাঁসর ঘণ্টা Public domain (PD-author release) A struck bell-metal gong, standing in for the kansar, the flat disc beaten during arati. It is not a kansar and we are not going to pretend it is. No recording of one exists under any licence we can use. A stand-in. This is a generic bell-metal gong, not a Bengali kansar. “Gong or Bell, vibrant” by stephan, originally from pdsounds.org, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons</data>
</node>
<node id="sound:43aab88f0b4d">
  <data key="d0">SoundscapeLayer</data>
  <data key="d61">A conch, blown once. Six and a half seconds, which is all a conch is, and the reason this fader sets how often it sounds rather than how loudly it drones. Recorded for the Wikipedia article on conches.</data>
  <data key="d66">CC BY 2.5 A conch, blown once. Six and a half seconds, which is all a conch is, and the reason this fader sets how often it sounds rather than how loudly it drones. Recorded for the Wikipedia article on conches. “Conch shell” by David Bolton (User:Dbolton), via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.5</data>
  <data key="d9">শঙ্খ CC BY 2.5 A conch, blown once. Six and a half seconds, which is all a conch is, and the reason this fader sets how often it sounds rather than how loudly it drones. Recorded for the Wikipedia article on conches. “Conch shell” by David Bolton (User:Dbolton), via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.5</data>
</node>
<node id="sound:f28908f91bf6">
  <data key="d0">SoundscapeLayer</data>
  <data key="d61">Temple bells in Delhi, recorded for the radio aporee sound map. Irregular, overlapping, a dozen hands at once, which is what a temple actually sounds like and what a single sampled bell never does.</data>
  <data key="d66">Public Domain Mark 1.0 Temple bells in Delhi, recorded for the radio aporee sound map. Irregular, overlapping, a dozen hands at once, which is what a temple actually sounds like and what a single sampled bell never does. “14, New Moti Bagh, New Delhi, Delhi 110023, India – 09 Temple bells” by Piotrek Zyla, radio aporee ::: maps, via the Internet Archive, Public Domain Mark 1.0</data>
  <data key="d9">মন্দিরের ঘণ্টা Public Domain Mark 1.0 Temple bells in Delhi, recorded for the radio aporee sound map. Irregular, overlapping, a dozen hands at once, which is what a temple actually sounds like and what a single sampled bell never does. “14, New Moti Bagh, New Delhi, Delhi 110023, India – 09 Temple bells” by Piotrek Zyla, radio aporee ::: maps, via the Internet Archive, Public Domain Mark 1.0</data>
